{
  "id": "Compound::DB00741 - Disease::DOID:9074",
  "verbalization": "Hydrocortisone is implicated in the pathogenesis of Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) through multiple indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that Hydrocortisone may contribute to SLE through its role in causing Adrenocortical Insufficiency Acute or Proximal Myopathy, both of which are treated by Betamethasone. Betamethasone, in turn, is linked to SLE, indicating a potential causal or therapeutic relationship. The lowest common ancestors of these pathways—17Alpha-Hydroxy Steroid—suggest a shared biological mechanism, possibly involving steroid hormone regulation. The evidence also highlights that Hydrocortisone may act as a precursor or mediator in the development of SLE, though the exact mechanism remains unclear. The integration of these pathways underscores the complex interplay between steroid hormones and autoimmune diseases, emphasizing the importance of understanding how corticosteroids influence immune responses and tissue inflammation.",
